Golang provides a strong foundation for concurrency control. 

Goroutines work like lightweight threads, and channels facilitate communication between them. 

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
)

func main() {
    ch := make(chan int)
    go func() {
        ch <- 5
    }()
    fmt.Println(<-ch)
}
```. This example demonstrates the basic usage of channels. 

For more complex examples, 
```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "time"
)

func worker(id int, ch chan int) {
    fmt.Printf("Worker %d started
", id)
    time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)
    ch <- id
}

func main() {
    ch := make(chan int)
    for i := 1; i <= 5; i++ {
        go worker(i, ch)
    }
    for i := 1; i <= 5; i++ {
        id := <-ch
        fmt.Printf("Worker %d finished
", id)
    }
}
```. This example shows how 5 worker goroutines communicate with the main goroutine through channels.
